(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the 'Pebble Smartwatch' vs. 'Apple Watch' evolution. How did Apple apply human-centred design to transform wearable computing from a niche developer gadget into an ubiquitous consumer product?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Pebble's Early Triumph & Geek-Centred Design \(2012\):) Tj
T*
(   - Pebble pioneered the modern smartwatch via Kickstarter, focusing on extreme functional) Tj
T*
(   utility: 7-day battery life, sunlight-readable e-paper screen, physical buttons, and an open C) Tj
T*
(   developer SDK.) Tj
T*
(   - It captivated technology enthusiasts and software developers but remained culturally and) Tj
T*
(   aesthetically niche.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Apple's Human-Centred Paradigm Shift \(2015\):) Tj
T*
(   - Reframing Wearables as Fashion & Health: Apple recognized that a wristwatch is an intimate) Tj
T*
(   piece of jewelry worn on the human body, not an accessory computer screen. They offered diverse) Tj
T*
(   casing sizes, materials \(aluminum, steel, gold\), and easily interchangeable bands.) Tj
T*
(   - The Taptic Engine & Glanceable UI: Rather than replicating a miniature iPhone screen with) Tj
T*
(   complex interactions, Apple engineered subtle physical haptic feedback that mimicked a human) Tj
T*
(   tap on the wrist, minimizing cognitive distraction.) Tj
T*
(   - Holistic Health & Life-Safety Ecosystem: Empathizing with deep human fears regarding) Tj
T*
(   mortality, Apple pivoted the core value proposition to ECG heart monitoring, fall detection,) Tj
T*
(   and emergency crash calling.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Strategic Takeaway:) Tj
T*
(   - Functional efficiency \(battery life\) alone does not win mainstream consumer markets;) Tj
T*
(   emotional resonance, ergonomic empathy, and socio-cultural alignment determine mass adoption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 19:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the '5 Whys' root-cause inquiry methodology developed by Sakichi Toyoda. Apply it to an engineering incident where a critical production database suffered catastrophic data corruption.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Methodological Framework:) Tj
T*
(   - Formulated by Sakichi Toyoda for the Toyota Production System, the '5 Whys' technique) Tj
T*
(   involves drilling down into a problem by asking 'Why?' five successive times.) Tj
T*
(   - The objective is to peel away superficial technical symptoms and expose the underlying human,) Tj
T*
(   procedural, and organizational root cause.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Production Database Corruption Case Study:) Tj
T*
(   - Problem: Production customer database dropped all foreign key constraints, corrupting active) Tj
T*
(   transaction logs.) Tj
T*
(   - Why 1: Why did the database drop the constraints? Because an automated schema migration) Tj
T*
(   script executed an unchecked `ALTER TABLE DROP CONSTRAINT` statement.) Tj
T*
(   - Why 2: Why did the migration script execute unchecked? Because the developer deployed it) Tj
T*
(   directly from their local laptop without running the staging verification pipeline.) Tj
T*
(   - Why 3: Why was the developer able to deploy directly to production? Because production) Tj
T*
(   database master credentials were hardcoded in a shared local configuration file.) Tj
T*
(   - Why 4: Why were production credentials hardcoded and accessible? Because the engineering team) Tj
T*
(   lacked a centralized secret management service \(Vault\) and automated IAM role enforcement.) Tj
T*
(   - Why 5: Why did the team lack secrets management? Because leadership prioritized immediate) Tj
T*
(   feature velocity over security infrastructure, failing to allocate sprint capacity for platform) Tj
T*
(   engineering governance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

